  "query_preview": "..\n\nTuesday through Friday night: An active weather pattern this week. A \nslow moving frontal band will bring light stratiform precipitaiton \nover the southern to central ID Panhandle today. Snow levels will \ndrop down to around 4000 feet with non-accumulating snow over \nLookout Pass. The exiting front leaves a moist boundary layer over \nthe Columbia Basin into the mountain valleys of northeast WA. Main \nconcern for dense fog will be in the Moses Lake Area where a Dense \nFog Advisory is in effect until 1100 AM Monday. There is the \npotential for fog to develop across the the northern portions of the \nbasin into the northern mountain valleys as well as the frontal band \nslowly nudges eastward overnight into Tuesday morning.\n\nA low pressure system on Wednesday tacks from the eastern Pacific \ntoward northern California. Even though this is where the bulk of \nthe dynamics will track, a deformation band swinging northward out \nahead of the low will bring the potential for light precipitation to \nat least the southern portions of the forecast area. Northeast flow \ninto the central portion of the Cascade Mountains will also bring \nfavorable upslope flow to Mission Ridge and over Blewett Pass. Those \ntraveling along Highway 97 south of Leavenworth over Blewett Pass \nwill see the potential for 2-4 inches of snow. Mission Ridge will \nsee the potential for over 4 inches of snowfall. The deformation \nband loses its punch further north across the region and it's \nunlikely that there will be enough moisture for impacts away \nfrom this small portion of the region Wednesday night into \nThursday morning. Temperatures trend cooler through the work \nweek down to around normal for mid to late November. High \ntemperatures by Thursday into Friday are expected to be in the \nupper 30s to low 40s with lows in the 20s.\n\nSaturday through Monday: The cooler temperatures and the \nprospects of another upper level low pressure system to move in \noff of the eastern Pacific will set up the potential for more \nwidespread lowland snowfall over the weekend. How much snow \naccumulates will depend on the timing of the moisture, but \nmodels are trending with a deformation band to push across \nSaturday night. There is uncertainty with how close the upper \nlow will track toward the Northwest. It may take similar tracks \nas previous lows typical of El Nino winters and dive further \nsouth, but the consensus is for this low to track more off of \nthe coast of WA and OR with greater moisture content with the \ndeformation band compared to the low around mid week. Snow \naccumulations are expected to be light but ranges in the 1 to 3 \ninch range are within reason. Check back throughout this week \nfor updates as the weekend approaches. /SVH",
